      "title": "Sizing the pool",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "- Each Ledger instance runs a pool of at most 20 connections.\n- The database accepts 400 connections in total.\n- 40 connections are reserved for migrations and administrators.\n- This leaves room for 18 instances at full size.\n- The autoscaler is capped at 18 instances.\n- Raising the pool size only helps while requests are waiting for a connection.\n- Once the database CPU is saturated, more connections make every query slower.\n- A pool sized for peak traffic also holds idle connections at night, which costs memory on the database but nothing else."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n2",
      "type": "text",
      "origin": "content",
      "title": "Timeouts",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "- A request waits at most 2 seconds to borrow a connection before it fails with `POOL_EXHAUSTED`.\n- The wait limit is deliberately shorter than the 5-second request deadline, so the caller still has time to return a clear error.\n- Each query also has a statement timeout of 3 seconds, set with `SET statement_timeout = '3s'` when the connection is opened.\n- The statement timeout does not apply to migrations, which run on their own reserved connections without a limit.\n- A long transaction holds its connection for its whole duration, so the team keeps transactions under 500 ms."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n3",
      "type": "text",
      "origin": "content",
      "title": "Health checks and failover",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "- Before lending a connection that has been idle for more than 60 seconds, the pool runs `SELECT 1` to check that it still works.\n- A connection that fails the check is closed and replaced, and the request gets a fresh one.\n- During a database failover, every open connection breaks at once.\n- The pool is drained and refilled from the new primary.\n- Refilling is limited to 5 new connections per second per instance, so that 18 instances reconnecting together don't overload the new primary.\n- Until the pool is refilled, requests may see `POOL_EXHAUSTED` errors for up to about 4 seconds."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n4",
      "type": "text",
      "origin": "content",
      "title": "Connection leaks",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "- A connection that stays borrowed for more than 10 seconds is logged as a possible leak, together with the stack trace of the code that borrowed it.\n- The pool does not take a leaked connection back by force, because the code holding it may still be using it.\n- Most leaks the team has found came from error paths that returned early without releasing the connection.\n- Leak warnings are reviewed every week, and a leak that comes back gets a ticket for the team that owns the code."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n5",
      "type": "text",
      "origin": "content",
      "title": "Watching the pool",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "- The pool exports three gauges: connections in use, idle connections, and requests waiting to borrow.\n- An alert pages the on-call engineer when requests have been waiting for more than 30 seconds in a row.\n- A sustained queue usually means slow queries rather than a pool that is too small, so the runbook starts with the slowest statements, not with the pool size.\n- The idle gauge drops to zero during a failover and recovers as the pool refills, which makes it a quick way to confirm that a failover has finished."
